R01=dict(id='R01',slug='how-i-got-into-research',title='I Want to Tell You How I Got Into Research',
 hook='Most people do undergraduate research for a portfolio. I did it for a different reason.',
 abstract=[('BACKGROUND','A lot of people get into undergraduate research for the sole purpose of building a portfolio. But I saw it from a different perspective.'),
 ('METHODS','A drop year, and an individual who believed in me quite a lot.'),
 ('RESULTS','I joined college and forgot all the whys and hows of why I got there.'),
 ('CONCLUSIONS','These ideas would eventually become the foundation of my interest in research.')],
 keywords='undergraduate research; medical education; preventive medicine; nutrition',
 highlight=None,
 body=[
 ('h','Why I did not see it as a portfolio'),
 'A lot of people get into undergraduate research for the sole purpose of building a portfolio so that they can go abroad, write the USMLE exam, or see it as a clearance aspect for their PLAB or AMC exams.',
 'But I saw it from a different perspective.',
 'It all started with how I got into MBBS in the first place.',
 ('h','The person who believed in me'),
 'I studied at PC Thomas Classes, and just before my drop year, I was influenced by an individual who believed in me quite a lot.',
 'He told me all about whole-food, plant-based diets and veganism. He was a vegan himself. He talked to me about Dr. Michael Greger, Neal Barnard, and the greats who had been working on preventive medicine and the Physicians Committee for Responsible Medicine.',
 'This was even before I understood the nuances of a medical college or had even seen a medical college for the first time.',
 'He showed me Cowspiracy, Seaspiracy and all these movies that showed us what happens when you consume a lot of meat and other things that, in several instances, didn’t have solid scientific data behind them.',
 'I was skeptical about a lot of this, but yet I listened to him because whole-food, plant-based diets and whole-food meals did have substance to them. And I kept that close to my heart.',
 ('h','The crossroads'),
 'I was at a crossroads in my life. I was very infuriated and washed away by the amount of time invested into NEET UG preparation during my 11th and 12th-grade schooling. I had enough of it.',
 'After my first attempt, I had enough marks to get into veterinary medicine or BDS, and I had decided that I would take whatever I got.',
 'But this individual and what he told me about whole-food diets, the power of preventive medicine and the influence of nutrition on life got me thinking.',
 'I told my mom and dad that I wanted to give it another go. And that’s how I decided to drop and repeat for an MBBS seat.',
 'I didn’t know then that these ideas would eventually become the foundation of my interest in research.',
 ('h','Then I joined college, and forgot'),
 'When I joined college, I was actually consumed by the need to fit in, to be validated, to be understood, to be loved, and to gain a place among my peers.',
 'I discussed these ideas with some of them in the initial days, but I was ridiculed for a while. I guess I just pushed them all out and focused on the things that were happening and very much the hype at the moment.',
 'Being isolated from college for a while due to COVID, then being back home, then back again, getting COVID twice, and trying to understand college dynamics, politics, arts and all the nuances of college life took up a lot of my attention.',
 'I kind of let all of that aside.',
 'I forgot about that guardian angel who actually took me to medical college. I forgot all the whys and hows of why I got there.',
 'I put a lot of time into politics, arguing, understanding college dynamics and things like that.',
 'But the overall atmosphere, when I look back, did not foster much academic thinking or even discourse.',
 'There were a lot of pretty faces, a lot of pretty decisions that are a regret, but you could say that all these decisions led me to where I am right now.',
 ])
R02=dict(id='R02',slug='nutrition-oncology-and-the-ncd-project',title='The First Research Questions: Nutrition, Oncology and the NCD Project',
 hook='I won a research award for a project I could never publish.',
 abstract=[('BACKGROUND','In second year, I went department by department, ward by ward, trying to understand oncology and nutrition and what was being done.'),
 ('METHODS','With Institutional Research Committee and Ethics Committee approval, we drafted a protocol based on the WHO STEPS 1 and 2 programme to assess risk factors for non-communicable diseases on campus.'),
 ('RESULTS','We prepared a report and presented it at national forums and undergraduate conferences. The project earned me the KUHS UG Research Award.'),
 ('CONCLUSIONS','We could never get around to publishing it. Far too much data.')],
 keywords='nutrition; oncology; non-communicable diseases; WHO STEPS; undergraduate research',
 highlight='1',
 body=[
 ('h','Second year: oncology and nutrition'),
 'While in second year, after certain setbacks and things that I never anticipated the way they would go, I took a few steps backward and reanalysed what I might have always wanted to do and what I wanted to focus on.',
 'I started going department by department, ward by ward, trying to understand oncology and nutrition and what was being done.',
 'I tried to understand if there was a nutritionist on board, why the diet books were always empty, and why the diet books were always filled up with things on renal-related diets and the diet was always just milk and eggs.',
 'Then I realised how a state like ours could only provide the set means, how protein is important, and how that is what the government is trying to do.',
 'But then again, I felt there was an imperative need to understand nutrition, assess the nutritional status of patients and their recovery, and understand the oncological status and so on.',
 'So I started reading.',
 'I started going into journals. I started looking up random terms in oncology, trying to understand what a review of literature was and what those nuances were.',
 'I started talking to radiation oncologists. They told me to read DeVita and Long’s oncology textbooks, which I did not particularly like, but yet I looked up how colon cancer and meat intake were related and how certain substances were classified as carcinogens.',
 'So who was assessing whether the people in Malabar were getting higher cancer rates due to this very rate of consumption?',
 'This was me in second year.',
 'For a while, I was focusing on these topics. I was trying to make a research project out of it, but I didn’t understand where to go or where to begin.',
 'I would talk to a professor, then to a JR, and they started writing it down.',
 'My dear professor and HOD, Dr. Asma Rahim, and Dr. Ashik Refeek told me to narrow it down.',
 'We started making it into nutritional assessment of patients, and then we modified it to cancer patients.',
 'I took this very project to my Medical Oncology professor, Dr. Sanudev. He told me we could do it, and then I let go of that project for a while.',
 ('h','The NCD project'),
 'It was third year. The academic pressure was getting to me. The conventional academic pressure. And I was never always just studying. I was always doing a couple of things at the same time, getting into politics, literature and everything that consumed me whole.',
 'Nearing third year, due to several setbacks, it was a difficult stretch. But yet, I saw an opportunity manifest before my eyes.',
 'It was an initiative by the then College Union to run a health camp.',
 'A lot of things came back to me, and I started thinking: a lot of data would come in, a lot of tests would be done. Why waste it if we could assess the health metrics and risk factors of all the students on campus?',
 'And that was how we drafted a protocol based on the WHO STEPS 1 and 2 programme, where we assess the risk factors for NCDs.',
 'I drafted a proposal based on that, made the protocol, and we decided to collect the data and carry out a STEPS 1 and 2 programme for assessing the risk factors for non-communicable diseases.',
 'While this happened, we collected data in an unconventional manner, trying to sort through it and make sense of it, with Institutional Research Committee approval and Ethics Committee approval.',
 'Soon after, it took another one and a half years for me to get into the data, understand it and learn how to process it using SPSS and Excel formulas to segregate the data and break it down set by set.',
 'That took a lot of my time in the final year, particularly the eighth semester.',
 'We eventually managed to prepare a report and present it at national forums and undergraduate conferences in college.',
 'But we could never get around to publishing it because of the vast amount of data and the lack of mentorship and guidance on how to go about handling it.',
 ('c','To this day, I believe we spread ourselves too thin.'),
 ('c','Too much data. Far too much data. Unsegmented, without the necessary tools or the team to understand the data, structure it properly or even publish it.'),
 'The project eventually earned me the KUHS UG Research Award.',
 'But to my dismay, I was never able to publish it.',
 'That is something that will always bother me. And it is something that I will owe the people who made it possible, and the campus, for a while.',
 ])
